For a lab-based heat transfer and fluids course, teams were asked to make wind turbine blades based on previous labs, running at low wind speeds but optimizing for power. To find the optimal wind speed, the Weibull distribution was used to find the most frequent wind speed that occurs in the wind tunnel. 4.8 m/s was the most frequent wind speed, and therefore the chosen one, which corresponds closest to 9 Hz (5 m/s). 

After choosing the optimal wind speed, the RPM was chosen. The maximum allowed was 2000 RPM, so for safety, an RPM of 1500 was chosen. The arifoil was chosen next based on the highest CL/CD with the longest range of the angle-of-attack at which the CL/CD occurred. This was chosen to be the S1223, an airfoil used in wind turbine applications and best for low Reynolds number flow, an inherent part of low wind speed. The length constraint was 6 in and the team constrained the chord to 2 in at the base to maximize surface area for air flow. 

