Testing began by running the wind tunnel with an empty test section to establish a correlation between fan frequency and tunnel airspeed using pitot-static tube measurements. After securely installing the wind turbine and closing the access hatch, power curves were collected at wind speeds of 2.7, 3.9, 4.4, and 5.0 m/s, with approximately 20–30 measurements per speed. For each test, the torque brake voltage was initially set to zero, and the tunnel speed was gradually increased to the target value while monitoring RPM and current to remain below safety limits of 3000 RPM and 0.09 A. Once steady state was reached, the brake voltage was increased incrementally until the turbine stopped, allowing the system to stabilize at each step before recording data. Additional data were collected with smaller voltage increments when necessary to better capture behavior near stall. Power versus RPM curves were then generated for each wind speed to identify maximum power output and assess whether the turbine achieved optimal performance near the design conditions.

<p align="center">
  <img src="{{ 'assets/images/powercurve.png' | relative_url }}"  width="200">
  <i>Experimental Power Curve</i>
</p>

The results show a strong relationship between wind speed and power output, with a line of best fit demonstrating an excellent correlation and an  $\R^2$ value of 0.9974. As expected, the trend passes through the origin since no power is generated in the absence of airflow. Maximum measured power occurred at 5.0 m/s, suggesting that the optimal operating wind speed may not yet have been reached, although the limited number of data points makes extrapolation uncertain. While the data indicate a continual increase in power with wind speed, real-world turbine behavior is expected to peak near the design speed and decrease thereafter. Peak power occurred near 1000 RPM, below the design RPM of 1500, which may help explain why the measured peak power of approximately 0.9 W was lower than the predicted 1.6 W. Despite time constraints limiting testing above 5.0 m/s, the turbine operated safely and produced peak power close to the design wind speed of 4.8 m/s, indicating that the design was largely successful.

